PNT.registerModule({"id": "m08", "number": 8, "title": "PNT Architecture and Systems Engineering", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Explain why PNT architecture should begin with mission and stakeholder needs rather than a preferred technology.", "Use user stories, use cases and operational scenarios to expose normal, degraded and failure behaviour.", "Identify PNT-dependent assets, stakeholders, external systems and system boundaries.", "Derive measurable functional, performance, resilience, interface, environmental and assurance requirements.", "Use accuracy, integrity, availability and continuity appropriately when specifying PNT performance.", "Express outage, holdover and recovery needs using time-bounded and mission-driven requirements.", "Allocate system-level requirements to PNT sources, monitoring, fusion, applications and supporting infrastructure.", "Develop functional, logical and physical views of a PNT architecture.", "Map the end-to-end PNT dependency chain, including external services, and identify hidden common-mode failure paths across a system of systems.", "Evaluate architecture options using traceable trade studies rather than selecting technology by headline accuracy.", "Maintain traceability from operational need through requirements, architecture and planned verification evidence.", "Recognise common PNT requirements and architecture anti-patterns."], "lessons": [{"id": "from-resilient-components-to-an-engineered-pnt-system", "title": "From Resilient Components to an Engineered PNT System", "html": "<p>Module 7 showed how resilient PNT functions can be arranged to resist disruption, detect loss of trust, fail over safely and recover. Module 8 moves one level up. The challenge is now to design the right PNT system for a specific mission, rather than start with a preferred technology and try to justify it afterwards.</p>\n<p>Systems engineering provides the discipline for doing this. It connects stakeholder needs and operational scenarios to measurable PNT requirements, architecture decisions, interfaces, risks, degraded modes and verification evidence. The result should be traceable: an engineer should be able to explain why every important PNT function exists, which requirement it satisfies and how that requirement will eventually be demonstrated.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The central question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">How do we translate a real operational need into a PNT architecture whose performance, resilience, interfaces, dependencies and recovery behaviour are explicit, justified and testable?</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m08-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Explain why PNT architecture should begin with mission and stakeholder needs rather than a preferred technology.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use user stories, use cases and operational scenarios to expose normal, degraded and failure behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify PNT-dependent assets, stakeholders, external systems and system boundaries.</p></li>\n<li><p>Derive measurable functional, performance, resilience, interface, environmental and assurance requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use accuracy, integrity, availability and continuity appropriately when specifying PNT performance.</p></li>\n<li><p>Express outage, holdover and recovery needs using time-bounded and mission-driven requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Allocate system-level requirements to PNT sources, monitoring, fusion, applications and supporting infrastructure.</p></li>\n<li><p>Develop functional, logical and physical views of a PNT architecture.</p></li>\n<li><p>Map the end-to-end PNT dependency chain, including external services, and identify hidden common-mode failure paths across a system of systems.</p></li>\n<li><p>Evaluate architecture options using traceable trade studies rather than selecting technology by headline accuracy.</p></li>\n<li><p>Maintain traceability from operational need through requirements, architecture and planned verification evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise common PNT requirements and architecture anti-patterns.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m08-scope-boundary\">Scope boundary</h2>\n<p>This module teaches architecture and requirements engineering. Module 9 will take the resulting requirements and architecture into detailed verification, validation, test design and assurance evidence.</p>"}, {"id": "the-systems-engineering-flow", "title": "The Systems-Engineering Flow", "html": "<p>A useful PNT engineering flow is deliberately top-down. It begins with the mission and only introduces technology after the required capability and tolerances are understood.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MISSION / USER NEED → USE CASES → PNT DEPENDENCIES → RISKS → REQUIREMENTS → ARCHITECTURE → INTERFACES → VERIFICATION EVIDENCE</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The Systems-Engineering Flow\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image1.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineer insight</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Architecture is not the first step. If a team starts with “we need RTK”, “we need an INS” or “we need two GNSS receivers”, ask what operational requirement led to that choice.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "define-the-mission-and-stakeholders", "title": "Define the Mission and Stakeholders", "html": "<p>PNT requirements exist because another capability consumes position, navigation or time. The first job is therefore to understand the operational outcome and the people or systems that depend on it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Question</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What to establish</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Who uses the PNT output?</td>\n<td>Operator, autonomous controller, communications system, sensor, weapon/system function, data platform or external service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>What PNT capability is consumed?</td>\n<td>Position, velocity, attitude, navigation, frequency, absolute time, relative time or a combination.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Why is it needed?</td>\n<td>The operational function that fails, degrades or becomes unsafe if PNT is unsuitable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Where and when is it used?</td>\n<td>Geography, platform state, indoor/outdoor, maritime/air/land, contested environment, fixed/mobile, mission phase.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>How critical is it?</td>\n<td>Consequence of unavailable, degraded or misleading PNT.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Who owns the decision?</td>\n<td>Stakeholders responsible for risk acceptance, operational use, system design, support and assurance.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not specify PNT in isolation</p><div class=\"callout-body\">“Position accuracy shall be 1 m” is not yet a good requirement unless the operational purpose, coordinate frame, environment, confidence/statistical basis and consequence of exceeding it are understood.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "user-stories-and-use-cases", "title": "User Stories and Use Cases", "html": "<p>The RethinkPNT resilience material supplied for this course recommends using user stories and use cases to bridge between stakeholder need and rigorous system requirements. A use case describes actors, preconditions, triggers, normal success behaviour and alternative flows when things go wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-example-user-story\">4.1 Example user story</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">User story</p><div class=\"callout-body\">As an autonomous surface-platform controller, I need a trustworthy navigation solution throughout the mission so that the platform can remain inside its uthorized operating corridor and enter a safe mode if navigation integrity cannot be maintained.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m08-turn-the-story-into-operational-flows\">4.2 Turn the story into operational flows</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Flow</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Questions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Normal flow</td>\n<td>What PNT is available? What performance is required? Which functions consume it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degraded flow</td>\n<td>What happens if accuracy worsens, a correction link is lost or one source becomes unavailable?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Misleading-source flow</td>\n<td>What happens if a source remains available but conflicts with independent evidence?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Extended outage</td>\n<td>What capability is required after 10 s, 1 min, 10 min, 1 h or longer?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery flow</td>\n<td>What must be true before a returning source can influence the solution again?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: User Stories and Use Cases\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image2.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why alternative flows matter</p><div class=\"callout-body\">The normal success path often produces performance requirements. The alternative paths expose resilience, failover, alerting, degraded-mode and recovery requirements that are easily missed.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "establish-the-system-boundary", "title": "Establish the System Boundary", "html": "<p>Before writing requirements, decide what is inside the PNT system being engineered and what is external. The DHS/CISA Resilient PNT Conformance Framework distinguishes boundaries ranging from fundamental PNT measurements through integrated receivers to systems of systems. The exact boundary chosen changes which dependencies, controls and interfaces are the responsibility of the design.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-body\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Establish the System Boundary\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure></div></aside>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Physical sources and sensors: GNSS, INS, terrestrial radio, vision, odometry, clocks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Antennas, RF distribution, cabling and power.</p></li>\n<li><p>Correction, timing and reference services.</p></li>\n<li><p>Networks, cloud services and communications links.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT processing, fusion, monitoring and orchestration.</p></li>\n<li><p>Interfaces to consuming applications and operators.</p></li>\n<li><p>External reference data, maps, calibration and configuration.</p></li>\n<li><p>Supplier support, update and maintenance paths.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m08-the-boundary-does-not-remove-external-dependencies\">5.1 The boundary does not remove external dependencies</h2>\n<p>Drawing a system boundary establishes engineering responsibility, but it does not make dependencies outside that boundary disappear. A PNT service can remain operationally dependent on external timing references, correction services, communications, maps, calibration data, power, software updates or supplier-operated infrastructure.</p>\n<p>A useful architecture therefore shows both the system boundary and the important dependencies that cross it. For each external dependency, record what is consumed, who provides it, how failure or corruption would affect the mission, whether an independent alternative exists, and how the system detects loss of trust.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Boundary test</p><div class=\"callout-body\">If failure of something outside your diagram can invalidate the PNT output, it is still an engineering dependency even if it is not under your direct control.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "map-the-pnt-dependency-chain-and-assets-at-risk", "title": "Map the PNT Dependency Chain and Assets at Risk", "html": "<p>Create an inventory of systems and functions that produce, distribute, transform or consume PNT. The Royal Institute of Navigation resilience checklist explicitly asks organisations to identify PNT-connected systems, why they need PNT, the effect of degradation or loss and their supplier dependencies. The resulting view should extend beyond the component that directly provides PNT to the application.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-follow-the-capability-upstream-and-downstream\">6.1 Follow the capability upstream and downstream</h2>\n<p>For each critical PNT-dependent function, trace the capability upstream to the physical or logical references on which it ultimately depends, and downstream to the functions that consume or act on it. This is the PNT dependency chain.</p>\n<p>A positioning chain might include: source or reference → signal or measurement → antenna/sensor → receiver → correction or communications service → PNT processor → application.</p>\n<p>A timing chain might include: external reference → receiver or timing source → local oscillator/clock → timing gateway → distribution network → consuming system.</p>\n<p>The same dependency can appear in several apparently independent paths. For example, two receivers may use different satellite constellations but share an antenna, power supply, correction network or upstream timing reference. Mapping the chain makes these common-mode dependencies visible before the architecture is treated as resilient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-questions-to-ask\">Questions to ask</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What upstream source or reference ultimately establishes the position or time?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which infrastructure, networks, data, power and supplier services are required between that source and the user?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which dependencies are shared by nominally independent PNT paths?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens if a dependency remains available but becomes incorrect, stale or untrusted?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which downstream functions become unsafe or ineffective when the PNT output is degraded, delayed or misleading?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Asset / dependency</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Role</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Failure question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Primary PNT source</td>\n<td>Provides normal position/time/navigation</td>\n<td>What happens if it is absent or misleading?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Alternative source</td>\n<td>Maintains or cross-checks capability</td>\n<td>Is it genuinely independent of the primary source?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Clock / oscillator</td>\n<td>Supports timing and holdover</td>\n<td>How does error grow without external discipline?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Correction service</td>\n<td>Improves accuracy/integrity</td>\n<td>What happens if corrections are stale, delayed or wrong?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network / data link</td>\n<td>Moves PNT or corrections</td>\n<td>What latency/loss/security assumptions exist?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Fusion / PNT manager</td>\n<td>Selects, combines and monitors sources</td>\n<td>Can one bad source contaminate the fused output?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Consuming application</td>\n<td>Uses PNT to make decisions</td>\n<td>Does it understand uncertainty, integrity and degraded modes?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "derive-requirements-from-operational-need", "title": "Derive Requirements from Operational Need", "html": "<p>A good PNT requirement states what the system must achieve under defined conditions. It should not prematurely prescribe the implementation unless the technology itself is genuinely constrained by the programme.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-requirement-categories\">7.1 Requirement categories</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Category</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Examples of what to specify</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Functional</td>\n<td>Provide position, velocity, attitude, UTC-aligned time, frequency, source-health state, integrity alert.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>Accuracy, uncertainty, integrity, availability, continuity, update rate, latency, time-to-first-fix, drift.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Resilience</td>\n<td>Threat/failure conditions to tolerate, holdover duration, degraded capability, failover and recovery behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interface</td>\n<td>Coordinate/reference frame, time scale, units, protocol, update rate, quality/status fields, alert semantics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Environmental</td>\n<td>Temperature, vibration, dynamics, RF environment, visibility, geography, indoor/outdoor, platform installation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Security / assurance</td>\n<td>Authentication, configuration control, logging, update provenance, supplier evidence, integrity monitoring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Support / lifecycle</td>\n<td>Calibration, maintenance, software updates, obsolescence, re-test triggers and technology refresh.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m08-make-performance-requirements-complete\">7.2 Make performance requirements complete</h2>\n<p>For a PNT performance requirement, consider at least: the quantity being measured, threshold, statistical confidence, reference truth, operating conditions, duration, update/latency requirement and required behaviour if the threshold is exceeded.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Weak requirement</p><div class=\"callout-body\">“The system shall provide accurate position during GNSS outages.”</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Stronger requirement example</p><div class=\"callout-body\">“Following loss of all GNSS measurements, the navigation service shall maintain horizontal position error within the mission-defined threshold for the specified bridging period, while indicating degraded mode and providing an uncertainty estimate to the consuming application.” The actual threshold and duration must come from the use case and risk analysis.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "specify-the-pnt-performance-attributes", "title": "Specify the PNT Performance Attributes", "html": "<p>Modules 1 and 3 introduced the core PNT performance concepts. Systems engineering turns them into explicit acceptance criteria.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Attribute</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Requirement question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>How close must the reported value be to truth, and with what statistical confidence?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>How confident must we be that the information is safe to use, and how quickly must an alert be raised when it is not?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>For what proportion of required operating time must the service meet its defined performance?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>What probability or tolerance applies to unexpected interruption during the operation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Latency</td>\n<td>How old may the PNT information be when consumed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Update rate</td>\n<td>How frequently must a new solution be provided?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Holdover / drift</td>\n<td>How far may error grow, and for how long, after an external reference is lost?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>How quickly must acceptable service be restored, and what revalidation is required?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Accuracy alone is insufficient</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A one-metre position that is ten seconds old, untrusted, in the wrong datum or unavailable at the critical moment may be useless. Specify the service the mission needs, not a single attractive number.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "resilience-requirements-threat-relative-and-time-bounded", "title": "Resilience Requirements: Threat-Relative and Time-Bounded", "html": "<p>Resilience is not a Boolean property. The supplied RethinkPNT paper stresses that it is a matter of degree against expected threats and required critical capabilities. The DHS/CISA framework similarly treats resilience as outcome-based behaviour selected according to application criticality and risk tolerance.</p>\n<p>A useful resilience requirement therefore combines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>The critical PNT capability that must survive.</p></li>\n<li><p>The threat, failure or adverse condition being considered.</p></li>\n<li><p>The required performance while the condition exists.</p></li>\n<li><p>The duration for which that performance must be sustained.</p></li>\n<li><p>The required detection and alert behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>The permitted degraded operating mode.</p></li>\n<li><p>The required recovery time and revalidation behaviour.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A useful statement pattern</p><div class=\"callout-body\">For application {X}, under condition/threat {Y}, the PNT service shall provide {capability Z} within {performance threshold} for {duration}, shall indicate {integrity/degraded state} within {detection time}, and shall recover to {normal acceptance criteria} within {recovery objective}.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m08-recovery-objectives\">9.1 Recovery objectives</h2>\n<p>The 2026 NIST PNT profile recommends objective recovery measures. In PNT terms, engineers can define a maximum tolerable PNT error during degraded or holdover operation and a Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for restoring acceptable service. These values must be derived from mission need rather than copied from another sector.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Resilience Requirements: Threat-Relative and Time-Bounded\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image4.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "allocate-requirements-to-the-architecture", "title": "Allocate Requirements to the Architecture", "html": "<p>System-level requirements must be allocated to the functions capable of satisfying them. This prevents a requirement such as “detect misleading PNT” from being left as an aspiration with no architectural owner.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>System requirement</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible allocation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Maintain navigation through primary-source outage</td>\n<td>INS/odometry propagation + uncertainty model + PNT manager mode logic.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detect inconsistent GNSS</td>\n<td>Receiver diagnostics + cross-source monitor + FDI logic.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Provide trusted time during network/GNSS loss</td>\n<td>Independent local clock + holdover model + alternate time source.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Alert application when integrity cannot be assured</td>\n<td>Integrity assessment + defined interface/status fields + application behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recover safely after source return</td>\n<td>Quarantine/revalidation logic + consistency window + controlled reintegration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Avoid single common-mode failure</td>\n<td>Physical/logical separation, diverse sources, independent power/network/reference paths.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Traceability question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">For every critical requirement, point to the architectural function that satisfies it. For every critical architectural function, point back to the requirement or risk that justifies it.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "functional-logical-and-physical-architecture-views", "title": "Functional, Logical and Physical Architecture Views", "html": "<p>One diagram rarely communicates everything. Use several complementary views.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>View</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Purpose</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical PNT content</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Functional</td>\n<td>What the system must do</td>\n<td>Acquire, monitor, compare, fuse, estimate uncertainty, alert, fail over, recover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Logical</td>\n<td>How functions exchange information</td>\n<td>Source health, measurements, corrections, state estimates, integrity status, commands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>Where functions are implemented</td>\n<td>Receivers, antennas, IMUs, clocks, processors, networks, servers, application hardware.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dependency / assurance</td>\n<td>What the system relies on</td>\n<td>Power, network, upstream GNSS, cloud, supplier services, reference data, operators.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Functional, Logical and Physical Architecture Views\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image5.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "worked-architecture-exercise-resilient-autonomous-surface-pl", "title": "Worked Architecture Exercise: Resilient Autonomous Surface Platform", "html": "<p>Consider an autonomous surface platform that must navigate within a defined operating area. GNSS is normally available, but the mission may encounter local interference and temporary communications loss. The platform must not continue autonomous manoeuvring on a misleading navigation solution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-1-operational-need\">12.1 Step 1: Operational need</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Maintain safe navigation during temporary loss of the primary external position source.</p></li>\n<li><p>Detect when externally referenced position becomes inconsistent with independent evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Provide the autonomy function with current PNT quality and integrity state.</p></li>\n<li><p>Restrict or terminate autonomous manoeuvring if required navigation integrity cannot be maintained.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recover normal operation only after the returning source is revalidated.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-2-candidate-requirements\">12.2 Step 2: Candidate requirements</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>ID</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative requirement</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-F-01</td>\n<td>The PNT service shall provide position, velocity, heading/attitude as required by the autonomy function, together with source-health and integrity state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-R-01</td>\n<td>The system shall detect loss or invalidity of the primary external position source and transition to the defined degraded navigation mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-R-02</td>\n<td>During the specified bridging interval, the PNT service shall propagate navigation using independent onboard sources while reporting increasing uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-I-01</td>\n<td>The application interface shall expose operating mode, solution age, uncertainty and integrity/alert state in addition to the PNT values.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-RC-01</td>\n<td>A previously suspect external source shall not be reintegrated until the defined revalidation criteria have been satisfied.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-A-01</td>\n<td>The design shall identify and mitigate single points and common modes capable of invalidating both primary and alternative navigation paths.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-3-candidate-architecture\">12.3 Step 3: Candidate architecture</h2>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Worked Architecture Exercise: Resilient Autonomous Surface Platform\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m08-image6.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-4-failure-behaviour\">12.4 Step 4: Failure behaviour</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Event</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Architecture response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GNSS hard loss</td>\n<td>Mark source unavailable; bridge using INS/other sources; uncertainty grows; application receives degraded state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>GNSS plausible but inconsistent</td>\n<td>Quarantine/exclude GNSS; preserve independent solution; raise integrity alert; do not let the bad source pull the fused state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Alternative source also degrades</td>\n<td>Move to restricted mode as thresholds are approached; autonomy limits capability or enters safe state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>GNSS returns</td>\n<td>Treat as recovering/suspect; compare against independent state for defined revalidation period; reintegrate only when criteria pass.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The architecture is derived, not decorative</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Each block exists because an operational flow, risk or requirement needs it. If a block cannot be traced to a need, challenge why it is present. If a requirement has no implementing function, the architecture is incomplete.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "interface-engineering-pnt-is-more-than-coordinates", "title": "Interface Engineering: PNT Is More Than Coordinates", "html": "<p>Many PNT failures become system failures at interfaces. The producer may know the solution is degraded while the consumer receives only latitude, longitude and time and continues operating normally.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Coordinate/reference frame and datum.</p></li>\n<li><p>Time scale and epoch, including UTC relationship where required.</p></li>\n<li><p>Units, sign conventions and axis definitions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Update rate, latency, timestamp and age of data.</p></li>\n<li><p>Accuracy/uncertainty representation and statistical meaning.</p></li>\n<li><p>Integrity or protection information and alert semantics.</p></li>\n<li><p>Source/mode status: normal, degraded, suspect, unavailable, recovering.</p></li>\n<li><p>Validity flags and behaviour when fields are stale or unavailable.</p></li>\n<li><p>Failure behaviour of the interface itself: loss, duplication, reordering or corruption.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warning\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Interface anti-pattern</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A high-quality PNT subsystem can still create an unsafe system if its uncertainty or integrity state is discarded at the interface to the application.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "architecture-trade-studies", "title": "Architecture Trade Studies", "html": "<p>There is rarely one universally best PNT architecture. Options should be compared against the same mission-derived criteria. Avoid selecting a technology because it has the best headline accuracy in ideal conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Trade criterion</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Questions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>Does it meet position/time/navigation performance in the actual environment?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Resilience</td>\n<td>Which threats/failures can it resist, detect, bridge and recover from?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Independence</td>\n<td>What upstream dependencies and common modes remain?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Where and when is the service available?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Infrastructure</td>\n<td>What external transmitters, reference stations, networks or cloud services are required?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>SWaP-C</td>\n<td>What size, weight, power and cost does the platform/system tolerate?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integration</td>\n<td>What interfaces, calibration, alignment and processing are required?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Maturity</td>\n<td>What is the technology readiness and operational evidence?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>Can performance and resilience claims be demonstrated under representative conditions?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Lifecycle</td>\n<td>How will it be maintained, updated, calibrated and refreshed?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Trade-study discipline</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Record assumptions and evidence. “Option A is more resilient” is too vague. State which threats, mission states and requirements drive the difference.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "traceability-and-the-pnt-requirements-matrix", "title": "Traceability and the PNT Requirements Matrix", "html": "<p>A simple traceability matrix can become the engineering spine of the PNT design. It links mission need to requirements, architecture and planned evidence.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Need / use case</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Requirement</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Architecture allocation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Risk / threat</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Verification method</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Navigate through short GNSS outage</td>\n<td>Maintain required position during bridging period</td>\n<td>INS + odometry + uncertainty propagation</td>\n<td>GNSS denial</td>\n<td>Test / analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Do not use misleading GNSS</td>\n<td>Detect and exclude inconsistent source</td>\n<td>Cross-source monitor + FDI + PNT manager</td>\n<td>Spoofing / receiver fault</td>\n<td>Test / demonstration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Maintain trusted timing</td>\n<td>Hold time within mission tolerance</td>\n<td>Independent clock + alternate timing</td>\n<td>GNSS/network loss</td>\n<td>Test / analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Restore normal service safely</td>\n<td>Revalidate returning source</td>\n<td>Recovery state + consistency criteria</td>\n<td>Residual corruption / bad reacquisition</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Prepare for Module 9</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Do not wait until testing to decide what evidence is needed. A requirement that cannot be objectively verified is a requirements problem before it becomes a test problem.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "architecture-and-requirements-anti-patterns", "title": "Architecture and Requirements Anti-Patterns", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Anti-pattern</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it is weak</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Better question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“Dual GNSS = resilient”</td>\n<td>Sources may share antenna, RF environment and failure modes.</td>\n<td>Which failures are actually independent?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“Centimetre accuracy required”</td>\n<td>No environment, confidence, integrity or operational reason.</td>\n<td>What mission decision needs that accuracy, under what conditions?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“Use INS as backup”</td>\n<td>No sensor grade, initialisation, drift or outage duration specified.</td>\n<td>What error is permitted after the required bridging time?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“Fail over if GPS fails”</td>\n<td>Failure and trigger are undefined; misleading data may remain available.</td>\n<td>What health/integrity conditions trigger exclusion and mode transition?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“Return to normal when GNSS returns”</td>\n<td>Returning data may still be untrustworthy.</td>\n<td>What revalidation criteria restore trust?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“Independent timing source”</td>\n<td>May be GNSS-disciplined upstream.</td>\n<td>Trace the complete timing chain and common dependencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>“PNT interface = lat/long/time”</td>\n<td>Consumer cannot understand quality or degraded state.</td>\n<td>What uncertainty, integrity, freshness and mode information is required?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "worked-mini-exercise-turn-a-vague-need-into-requirements", "title": "Worked Mini-Exercise: Turn a Vague Need into Requirements", "html": "<p>Starting statement: “The system needs resilient timing because GNSS may be unavailable.”</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-1-ask-what-timing-supports\">Step 1: Ask what timing supports</h2>\n<p>Suppose timing is required to keep distributed equipment synchronised. Loss beyond a defined offset causes the service to stop meeting its operational requirement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-2-define-the-adverse-condition\">Step 2: Define the adverse condition</h2>\n<p>The external GNSS reference may be absent or untrusted for a mission-defined outage period. Network connectivity may also be unavailable, so the architecture cannot assume an internet time service is always present.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m08-step-3-derive-requirement-themes\">Step 3: Derive requirement themes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Maximum permitted time error relative to the required reference.</p></li>\n<li><p>How long that bound must be maintained after reference loss.</p></li>\n<li><p>How quickly loss or inconsistency must be detected.</p></li>\n<li><p>Whether an alternate independent reference is required.</p></li>\n<li><p>What degraded status must be reported downstream.</p></li>\n<li><p>What revalidation is required before an external reference can discipline the local clock again.</p></li>\n<li><p>What evidence will demonstrate holdover and recovery performance.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Result</p><div class=\"callout-body\">The technology choice now follows the requirement. The engineering team can evaluate oscillator/clock performance, independent time sources, distribution methods and monitoring against a defined operational problem.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-pnt-systems-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a PNT Systems Engineer", "html": "<p>When reviewing a PNT architecture or supplier proposal, useful questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What operational capability is this PNT requirement protecting?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which user stories and degraded/failure scenarios were used to derive the requirements?</p></li>\n<li><p>Where is the PNT system boundary, and what important dependencies sit outside it?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which requirements define accuracy, integrity, availability, continuity, latency and recovery?</p></li>\n<li><p>What threats or failures is the resilience claim relative to?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long must the capability survive each outage, and at what performance?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which architectural functions own detection, exclusion, failover, holdover and recovery?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which sources are genuinely independent when the specified failure occurs?</p></li>\n<li><p>What information reaches the consuming application about uncertainty, integrity and operating mode?</p></li>\n<li><p>How are supplier services, networks, power, reference data and software updates represented in the dependency model?</p></li>\n<li><p>What assumptions drove the architecture trade study?</p></li>\n<li><p>Can every critical requirement be traced to planned verification evidence?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>PNT systems engineering begins with mission need, stakeholders and operational scenarios, not a preferred technology.</p></li>\n<li><p>User stories and use cases help expose normal, degraded, failure and recovery behaviours before requirements are fixed.</p></li>\n<li><p>The PNT system boundary defines engineering scope, but external dependencies that can invalidate the delivered capability remain part of the assurance problem.</p></li>\n<li><p>Mapping the end-to-end PNT dependency chain exposes upstream references, supporting infrastructure, downstream consumers and hidden common-mode failure paths.</p></li>\n<li><p>Requirements should cover function, performance, resilience, interfaces, environment, security/assurance and lifecycle needs.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT performance requirements need defined conditions, reference truth, confidence and operational meaning.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resilience requirements are threat-relative and time-bounded; they should state what capability survives, for how long and at what performance.</p></li>\n<li><p>Requirements must be allocated to architecture functions such as source monitoring, FDI, fusion, holdover, application interfaces and recovery logic.</p></li>\n<li><p>Functional, logical, physical and dependency views reveal different aspects of the same PNT system.</p></li>\n<li><p>Architecture trade studies should compare options against common mission-derived criteria rather than headline technology claims.</p></li>\n<li><p>Traceability from need to requirement to architecture to verification evidence is central to an assessable PNT design.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Stakeholder need</td>\n<td>Outcome or capability required by a person, organisation or consuming system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>User story</td>\n<td>Short statement of a user/actor need and the value or outcome sought.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Use case</td>\n<td>Structured description of actors, preconditions, triggers, normal flow and alternative/failure flows.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>System boundary</td>\n<td>Defined scope separating the engineered system from external systems and dependencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Functional requirement</td>\n<td>Statement of what the system must do.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Performance requirement</td>\n<td>Quantified statement of how well a function must be delivered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Resilience requirement</td>\n<td>Required behaviour/performance when specified threats, failures or adverse conditions occur.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirement allocation</td>\n<td>Assignment of a system requirement to the architecture function/component responsible for satisfying it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Interface Control</td>\n<td>Definition of the information, format, timing, semantics and behaviour exchanged across an interface.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>System of systems</td>\n<td>Combination of constituent systems/services whose coordinated operation delivers the required capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Trade study</td>\n<td>Structured comparison of design options against defined criteria and assumptions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Traceability</td>\n<td>Ability to link needs, requirements, architecture, risks and evidence bidirectionally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RTO</td>\n<td>Recovery Time Objective: the required time within which acceptable service must be restored after disruption.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>Continued provision of time/navigation capability after loss of an external reference, with bounded/growing uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Common-mode dependency</td>\n<td>Shared dependency capable of causing multiple nominally separate sources/functions to fail together.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT dependency chain</td>\n<td>End-to-end set of sources, infrastructure, services, interfaces and consumers on which a PNT capability depends.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p><strong>RethinkPNT: PNT System Resilience</strong><br/>\nParticularly useful for user stories, use cases, requirements engineering, resilience functions and verification/assurance linkage.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>DHS / CISA: Resilient PNT Conformance Framework v2.0</strong><br/>\nOutcome-based resilient-PNT behaviours, user-equipment boundaries, resilience levels and stakeholder communication of measurable performance.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: PNT Resilience Checklist</strong><br/>\nPractical questions on PNT dependencies, independent sources, extended outages, mitigation and resilience testing.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST IR 8323 Rev. 2: Foundational PNT Profile</strong><br/>\nCurrent 2026 risk-management profile for systems using PNT, including dependency identification, third-party risks, monitoring, response and recovery.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing Overview</strong><br/>\nNational PNT Office overview and UK framework for greater PNT resilience.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Space Strategy</strong><br/>\nCurrent UK direction toward layered, independent PNT systems and a system-of-systems approach.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK MOD: Space Capability Management Plan</strong><br/>\nDefence context for assured and resilient global PNT using diverse space and terrestrial technologies without single points of failure.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-capability-management-plan/space-capability-management-plan\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-capability-management-plan/space-capability-management-plan</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Space-based PNT Technical Concepts</strong><br/>\nUseful example of why customer-endorsed SMART mission objectives and use cases are needed before evaluating PNT technical concepts.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: Introduction to PNT in the 21st Century</strong><br/>\nComplementary professional learning covering PNT foundations, resilient PNT, alternative technologies, reference frames, timing and future PNT concepts.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.pathlms.com/rin/courses/120419\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.pathlms.com/rin/courses/120419</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 09 — PNT VERIFICATION, VALIDATION &amp; ASSURANCE</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 9 takes the requirements, architecture and traceability developed here and asks how to prove the system does what is claimed. It will cover verification strategy, reference truth, test environments, nominal and degraded testing, denial/deception scenarios at a safe assurance level, failover and holdover testing, recovery and revalidation, performance statistics, evidence traceability and construction of a PNT assurance argument.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "What should normally drive a PNT architecture first?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The receiver with the best advertised accuracy."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Mission/user needs, operational scenarios and risk."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The cheapest GNSS chipset."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The number of available constellations."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Technology should be selected to satisfy mission-derived requirements, not used as the starting assumption."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "What can an alternative-flow use case expose?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Degraded-mode behaviour."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Failover requirements."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Integrity alerts."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Recovery behaviour."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Only normal accuracy."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Alternative flows reveal what the system must do when normal assumptions fail."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Why is defining the PNT system boundary important?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It determines which components, interfaces and dependencies the engineering responsibility includes."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It guarantees GNSS availability."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It removes the need for requirements."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It defines satellite orbit geometry."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "The boundary determines what the architecture owns and which external dependencies must still be managed."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Which are valid PNT requirement categories?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Functional."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Performance."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Resilience."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Interface."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Environmental and assurance."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "A complete specification covers behaviour, performance, resilience, interfaces, environment and assurance/lifecycle needs."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "Which is the strongest requirement statement?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The system shall be resilient."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The system shall use an INS."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Under a defined primary-source outage, the PNT service shall maintain the mission-defined performance for the required duration and indicate degraded state."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The receiver shall be accurate."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "It defines the adverse condition, required behaviour, duration/performance concept and state reporting rather than using vague resilience language."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "What should a PNT interface communicate where operationally required?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "PNT values."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Timestamp/age."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Uncertainty."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Integrity/mode state."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Validity/alerts."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Consumers often need quality and trust information as well as the raw PNT values."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "What is requirement allocation?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Assigning each system requirement to the functions/components responsible for satisfying it."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Choosing a vendor before defining the mission."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Counting satellites."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Removing traceability to simplify the design."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Allocation gives requirements an architectural owner and exposes gaps."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "Which should be considered in a PNT architecture trade study?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Performance in the intended environment."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Common-mode dependencies."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Infrastructure and communications."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Maturity and assurance evidence."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Lifecycle and integration constraints."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "A viable architecture balances mission performance, resilience, dependencies, integration, maturity, evidence and lifecycle constraints."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "Why is “return to normal when GNSS comes back” a weak recovery requirement?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "GNSS can never return."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A returning source may still be inconsistent or compromised and should meet revalidation criteria first."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Recovery is only relevant to timing."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It requires too many satellites."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Recovery is controlled re-establishment of trust, not automatic acceptance of the first returning data."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "What should traceability connect?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Operational need/use case."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Requirement."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Architecture allocation."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Risk/threat."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Planned verification evidence."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "End-to-end traceability connects why the capability is needed to how it is designed and how compliance will be demonstrated."}]});
